Me on 2011/8/3 AD

1594
00:39:34,267 --> 00:39:38,977
But, for example, why did you decide to hack SERN, Hashida?

1595
00:39:38,977 --> 00:39:41,507
Um, well...

1596
00:39:41,507 --> 00:39:44,567
It kind of felt like a super-exciting thing to do.

1597
00:39:44,567 --> 00:39:45,277
Is that all?

1598
00:39:45,277 --> 00:39:51,617
Wasn't there a part of you that felt you had to do something?

1599
00:39:51,617 --> 00:39:55,537
Mayuri, why do you always buy Dr Pepper?

1600
00:39:56,667 --> 00:39:59,777
Because I always think someone will drink it...

1601
00:39:59,777 --> 00:40:03,297
But neither Hashida nor I particularly like the drink.

1602
00:40:03,297 --> 00:40:05,537
Nor do you, Mayuri.

1603
00:40:05,537 --> 00:40:09,227
Yet you buy Dr Pepper every day.

1604
00:40:10,367 --> 00:40:13,247
Do I?

1605
00:40:13,247 --> 00:40:14,397
Something's...

1606
00:40:15,067 --> 00:40:17,227
Something isn't right...

1607
00:40:18,027 --> 00:40:19,397
Something's missing...

1608
00:40:18,527 --> 00:40:19,397
Cell phone
 Microwave oven

1609
00:40:19,767 --> 00:40:21,247
What?

1610
00:40:21,247 --> 00:40:23,907
I'm not sure. That's why...

1611
00:40:25,027 --> 00:40:26,317
No way!

1612
00:40:26,317 --> 00:40:28,787
Kurisu, are you going to use this?

1613
00:40:29,117 --> 00:40:33,847
Right. If I'm able to use this machine to go back in time,

1614
00:40:33,847 --> 00:40:38,907
I might seriously alter the world by changing the past.

1615
00:40:39,817 --> 00:40:43,587
But I can't leave this situation as it is now.

1616
00:40:43,587 --> 00:40:45,457
What do you wanna do?

1617
00:40:45,457 --> 00:40:49,257
I'll go back to the day before I went to that laundromat.

1618
00:40:49,257 --> 00:40:52,267
I more or less remember what I did that day.

1619
00:40:53,367 --> 00:40:56,307
Can you go back further than 48 hours?

1620
00:40:57,307 --> 00:41:00,227
Who said that the limit is 48 hours?

1621
00:41:00,737 --> 00:41:01,937
Oh?

1622
00:41:02,817 --> 00:41:04,947
Why did I think that?

1623
00:41:06,197 --> 00:41:11,277
I'll act according to my memory, minimizing my impact on the past.

1624
00:41:11,277 --> 00:41:12,957
I'll only be there

1625
00:41:13,217 --> 00:41:16,527
to trace the origin of this uneasy feeling.

1626
00:41:16,527 --> 00:41:18,287
But will it work?

1627
00:41:18,887 --> 00:41:20,827
I won't push it any further if it doesn't.

1628
00:41:20,827 --> 00:41:23,847
And I'll never use this machine again.

1629
00:41:24,257 --> 00:41:26,507
Neither should the two of you.

1630
00:41:31,697 --> 00:41:33,017
It's connected!

1631
00:41:33,547 --> 00:41:36,157
Are you sure about this, Miss Makise?

1632
00:41:41,487 --> 00:41:43,167
Hashida, please!

1633
00:41:43,167 --> 00:41:45,107
Okay. Let's do this!

1634
00:42:04,757 --> 00:42:07,587
Mix! Mix! Mix! Mix! Mix! Mix!

1635
00:42:04,757 --> 00:42:08,957
Wow! Eye-contact Mix Mix Yakisoba!

1636
00:42:11,557 --> 00:42:14,887
Oh! Nae, you can eat peppers?!

1637
00:42:15,627 --> 00:42:16,977
I'm impressed!

1638
00:42:16,977 --> 00:42:20,017
Yeah, but my daddy can't.

1639
00:42:20,017 --> 00:42:22,407
<i>Nae, Mayuri,</i>

1640
00:42:22,407 --> 00:42:25,277
<i>Hashida, Faris,</i>

1641
00:42:26,187 --> 00:42:31,617
<i>the Manager, Kiryuu, and Urushibara.</i>

1642
00:42:37,117 --> 00:42:38,577
Okabe!

1643
00:42:38,577 --> 00:42:41,287
Kurisu, what's wrong?

1644
00:42:41,287 --> 00:42:43,247
Is Okarin okay?

1645
00:42:44,427 --> 00:42:45,047
Okabe...

1646
00:42:46,267 --> 00:42:48,747
<i>Why did I forget about him?</i>

1647
00:42:48,747 --> 00:42:54,517
<i>No, I didn't forget. It was like he never existed...</i>

1648
00:42:59,977 --> 00:43:05,567
Now, as the founder of this lab I'll do the first toast of the night!

1649
00:43:05,567 --> 00:43:08,387
Let's first make sure that everyone's got a drink, nyan~!

1650
00:43:08,387 --> 00:43:11,017
Not ready yet? Cheers!

1651
00:43:11,677 --> 00:43:15,047
<i>It's like Okabe doesn't exist...</i>

1652
00:43:15,047 --> 00:43:16,907
Here.

1653
00:43:24,167 --> 00:43:27,227
Can Okarin eat shrimp?

1654
00:43:33,987 --> 00:43:35,097
Okabe!

1655
00:43:44,357 --> 00:43:47,167
<i>Looks like your time leap was successful.</i>

1656
00:43:50,247 --> 00:43:51,407
Who is this?

1657
00:43:51,407 --> 00:43:53,437
<i>The building on the right.</i>

1658
00:44:09,757 --> 00:44:13,407
In order to be able to speak to you, Makise Kurisu,

1659
00:44:13,407 --> 00:44:17,677
I needed you to become an observer of this world.

1660
00:44:18,887 --> 00:44:20,347
Observer?

1661
00:44:20,787 --> 00:44:23,687
By time-leaping into the past,

1662
00:44:23,687 --> 00:44:26,567
you were able to acknowledge the disappearance of Okabe Rintarou—

1663
00:44:26,567 --> 00:44:29,707
Wait! Before that, who are you?

1664
00:44:30,457 --> 00:44:34,247
Oh, sorry! You haven't met me yet!

1665
00:44:34,247 --> 00:44:36,387
My name is Amane Suzuha.

1666
00:44:37,117 --> 00:44:38,577
I came from the future

1667
00:44:39,287 --> 00:44:42,547
with the time machine built by the future you.

1668
00:44:43,557 --> 00:44:46,037
The time machine... I built?

1669
00:44:46,947 --> 00:44:49,407
Yes, it all sounds hard to believe,

1670
00:44:49,407 --> 00:44:55,547
but the Steins Gate world line is a great danger for Okabe Rintarou.

1671
00:44:56,497 --> 00:44:58,607
His memories of other world lines

1672
00:44:58,607 --> 00:45:00,557
are overloading his Reading Steiner

1673
00:45:00,557 --> 00:45:03,517
and it's becoming difficult for him to remain on this world line.

1674
00:45:04,627 --> 00:45:06,807
Then where is he?

1675
00:45:06,807 --> 00:45:09,147
He's most likely on the "R" world line.

1676
00:45:10,277 --> 00:45:16,487
It's only 0.000001% off from the one we're on now...

1677
00:45:17,457 --> 00:45:20,137
This is the sole world line that avoids both

1678
00:45:20,137 --> 00:45:24,307
the convergences of Shiina Mayuri's and Makise Kurisu's deaths.

1679
00:45:24,307 --> 00:45:28,457
That's why it's more unstable than others and fluctuates,

1680
00:45:28,457 --> 00:45:33,217
and there's the "R" world line just next to it.

1681
00:45:34,337 --> 00:45:36,587
The memories of the drifting world lines

1682
00:45:36,587 --> 00:45:39,487
overloading the Reading Steiner are causing

1683
00:45:39,487 --> 00:45:41,807
Okabe Rintarou to pick up on that fluctuation

1684
00:45:41,807 --> 00:45:44,687
and time-leap to the "R" world line.

1685
00:45:44,687 --> 00:45:50,247
...And it's as though he never existed on this one.

1686
00:45:50,247 --> 00:45:52,727
Is that the cause of my vertigo?

1687
00:45:53,687 --> 00:45:55,217
Okabe...

1688
00:45:55,217 --> 00:45:56,777
You were listening?

1689
00:45:56,777 --> 00:45:59,567
Yes, I can hardly believe it,

1690
00:46:00,247 --> 00:46:03,457
but if this is the word of the Part-Time Soldier, it must be true.

1691
00:46:03,457 --> 00:46:06,707
Part-time soldier? What's that?

1692
00:46:06,707 --> 00:46:12,707
No, on this world line you're neither a part-timer nor a soldier...

1693
00:46:12,707 --> 00:46:14,147
Okabe!

1694
00:46:22,407 --> 00:46:23,887
Okabe?

1695
00:46:32,157 --> 00:46:33,617
I'll disappear?

1696
00:46:33,617 --> 00:46:35,967
Yes... tomorrow morning

1697
00:46:35,967 --> 00:46:39,957
and then you will never have existed

1698
00:46:39,957 --> 00:46:41,947
in everyone's minds.

1699
00:46:43,267 --> 00:46:45,117
Never existed...

1700
00:46:45,697 --> 00:46:49,907
Powerful memories from other world lines act as triggers that cause

1701
00:46:49,907 --> 00:46:52,867
the Okabe Rintarou observing this world line as a reality

1702
00:46:52,867 --> 00:46:57,587
to sense these fluctuations and oscillate between lines himself.

1703
00:46:58,447 --> 00:47:01,297
Memory cannot be controlled...

1704
00:47:01,687 --> 00:47:02,897
That's troublesome.

1705
00:47:03,267 --> 00:47:05,567
No matter how much we may want to forget them,

1706
00:47:05,567 --> 00:47:09,527
memories we cannot forget remain, in spite of our will.

1707
00:47:10,437 --> 00:47:11,937
Especially for you, Okabe...

1708
00:47:12,257 --> 00:47:17,027
You experienced strong fear and sadness on the other world lines,

1709
00:47:17,377 --> 00:47:22,617
and the self-reproach you felt remains in your memory too.

1710
00:47:23,027 --> 00:47:26,577
Now you see the same people,

1711
00:47:26,577 --> 00:47:30,627
the same room, the same city, and the same words from your memories.

1712
00:47:30,627 --> 00:47:32,957
They'd be impossible to forget.

1713
00:47:32,957 --> 00:47:37,427
Memories of other world lines, which seem like dreams but aren't...

1714
00:47:37,977 --> 00:47:42,307
Memories that are real when they shouldn't be.

1715
00:47:42,307 --> 00:47:46,537
This ambiguity turns your awareness into a risk.

1716
00:47:47,197 --> 00:47:51,747
So you came from the future to save me?

1717
00:47:51,747 --> 00:47:52,867
You could say that.

1718
00:47:53,527 --> 00:47:57,857
The only way to save Okabe Rintarou is to go to the past with the time machine.

1719
00:47:58,657 --> 00:47:59,057
So...

1720
00:47:59,057 --> 00:47:59,897
I refuse!

1721
00:48:00,427 --> 00:48:02,647
Return to the future at once.

1722
00:48:02,647 --> 00:48:04,867
Then destroy the Time Machine right away.

1723
00:48:05,697 --> 00:48:09,907
It cannot be allowed to exist. Destroy it immediately!

1724
00:48:10,767 --> 00:48:11,867
Wait!

1725
00:48:12,137 --> 00:48:17,167
I was finally able to come here with the time-leap machine!

1726
00:48:18,977 --> 00:48:22,877
What?! Don't tell me that you... The time-leap...!

1727
00:48:24,857 --> 00:48:29,327
I built it with Hashida using the cell phone, the microwave oven, and...

1728
00:48:29,327 --> 00:48:30,847
Why did you do that?!

1729
00:48:30,847 --> 00:48:34,397
Time-leap machines and time machines should never be built!

1730
00:48:34,777 --> 00:48:37,897
Even if you can figure out how to do it,

1731
00:48:37,897 --> 00:48:40,157
you should never ever build one!

1732
00:48:40,627 --> 00:48:42,607
Okabe... you're hurting me!

1733
00:48:45,037 --> 00:48:46,117
Sorry...

1734
00:48:48,647 --> 00:48:52,117
But if I don't do something, you'll vanish.

1735
00:48:52,917 --> 00:48:56,397
You will never have existed...

1736
00:48:56,867 --> 00:48:59,797
I have no choice but to change the past—

1737
00:48:59,797 --> 00:49:01,507
And what if you fail?

1738
00:49:04,507 --> 00:49:07,227
What if it doesn't go well?

1739
00:49:10,397 --> 00:49:12,057
The answer is simple.

1740
00:49:12,397 --> 00:49:13,937
You do it again.

1741
00:49:13,937 --> 00:49:17,847
You keep going back to the past until you succeed.

1742
00:49:17,847 --> 00:49:23,737
As long as they have the means to go back in time, that's what people will do.

1743
00:49:23,737 --> 00:49:27,117
But it'll only increase the suffering.

1744
00:49:27,887 --> 00:49:32,657
A change in the past will always affect something else.

1745
00:49:33,097 --> 00:49:37,127
It will never change in a way that's convenient for you.

1746
00:49:37,957 --> 00:49:39,377
If you save someone,

1747
00:49:39,707 --> 00:49:41,667
you will lose someone...

1748
00:49:42,137 --> 00:49:45,217
Dreams you finally fulfilled won't exist anymore.

1749
00:49:45,217 --> 00:49:48,257
Your long-held desires will be snatched away, eradicated...

1750
00:49:48,257 --> 00:49:53,177
And when the change you hoped for doesn't happen,

1751
00:49:53,177 --> 00:49:55,887
you will have to keep facing that inescapable reality

1752
00:49:55,887 --> 00:49:58,967
again and again, again and again!

1753
00:49:59,767 --> 00:50:02,147
Do you realize the pain of continuously repeating all of that,

1754
00:50:02,977 --> 00:50:06,487
while bearing the responsibility for all those losses?

1755
00:50:06,487 --> 00:50:12,177
Do you understand the fear of losing your humanity after it wears you down?

1756
00:50:12,987 --> 00:50:13,787
But...

1757
00:50:13,787 --> 00:50:17,787
Even if you have the means, the past must not be changed.

1758
00:50:18,787 --> 00:50:21,667
You must not turn chance into reality.

1759
00:50:22,187 --> 00:50:24,607
No one knows the future.

1760
00:50:24,607 --> 00:50:28,977
It's because the past cannot be undone that people can accept all sorts of pain,

1761
00:50:28,977 --> 00:50:32,097
adversity, and cruel accidents, yet still move forward.

1762
00:50:32,097 --> 00:50:33,807
Then what are you going to do, Okabe?

1763
00:50:35,127 --> 00:50:38,607
You're going to disappear!

1764
00:50:39,217 --> 00:50:41,627
I... saw it...

1765
00:50:42,267 --> 00:50:45,307
A world where you don't exist...

1766
00:50:45,307 --> 00:50:48,777
A lab with only Mayuri, Hashida, and me...

1767
00:50:49,417 --> 00:50:52,237
Where nobody remembers you...

1768
00:50:53,017 --> 00:50:55,997
It was crueler than death!

1769
00:50:55,997 --> 00:50:59,367
Any mark or meaning of your existence will disappear!

1770
00:51:03,997 --> 00:51:05,167
I don't care.

1771
00:51:08,567 --> 00:51:09,987
Okabe...

1772
00:51:09,987 --> 00:51:14,647
All I wanted was for you and Mayuri to live in peace

1773
00:51:14,647 --> 00:51:16,747
and keep walking towards the future.

1774
00:51:16,747 --> 00:51:18,057
Nothing more.

1775
00:51:18,747 --> 00:51:20,867
It's fine as long as that comes true.

1776
00:51:20,867 --> 00:51:25,107
What you're saying now, the things you've accomplished,

1777
00:51:25,107 --> 00:51:27,777
even your name... We'll forget everything!

1778
00:51:28,237 --> 00:51:31,407
But Mayuri won't die, and you can keep on living too.

1779
00:51:52,397 --> 00:51:54,097
How long will you go on following me?

1780
00:51:55,597 --> 00:51:58,387
I'm not! I'm just going to the station.

1781
00:51:59,277 --> 00:52:00,937
But your hotel's not this way.

1782
00:52:00,937 --> 00:52:02,147
I've got somewhere to go.

1783
00:52:02,497 --> 00:52:03,487
Where?

1784
00:52:03,487 --> 00:52:05,857
It's my business, not yours.

1785
00:52:31,287 --> 00:52:33,257
I'm warning you.

1786
00:52:33,527 --> 00:52:35,097
What?

1787
00:52:35,097 --> 00:52:38,577
The moment I disappear tomorrow,

1788
00:52:38,577 --> 00:52:43,567
the people around you will think I never existed,

1789
00:52:44,247 --> 00:52:47,017
but since you came here with a time-leap,

1790
00:52:47,797 --> 00:52:49,927
you may still remember me.

1791
00:52:50,697 --> 00:52:53,147
Yes... I suppose so.

1792
00:52:53,427 --> 00:52:58,157
To a small degree, Reading Steiner is present in every human being.

1793
00:52:58,667 --> 00:53:01,537
Since you've been leaping through time,

1794
00:53:01,537 --> 00:53:04,077
you may experience it more than others.

1795
00:53:04,077 --> 00:53:06,637
What are you trying to say?

1796
00:53:07,927 --> 00:53:09,187
Forget.

1797
00:53:10,507 --> 00:53:12,647
Forget me.

1798
00:53:12,647 --> 00:53:16,657
Just like the others, think that I never existed.

1799
00:53:17,207 --> 00:53:19,137
This moment was just a dream.

1800
00:53:20,887 --> 00:53:25,937
Remembering something that no one else can is a painful thing.

1801
00:53:26,807 --> 00:53:31,147
You can't talk to anyone about it. No one will understand you.

1802
00:53:31,647 --> 00:53:32,687
You'll be alone.

1803
00:53:34,027 --> 00:53:37,407
So that's how it's been for you all this time?

1804
00:53:39,567 --> 00:53:40,667
Then...

1805
00:53:43,607 --> 00:53:44,617
Kurisu...

1806
00:53:45,407 --> 00:53:48,207
Then... it's no good.

1807
00:53:48,817 --> 00:53:50,747
You have to exist!

1808
00:53:59,177 --> 00:54:02,507
If that's what I did for you on another world line,

1809
00:54:03,537 --> 00:54:05,797
then do the same for me!

1810
00:54:05,797 --> 00:54:08,807
Stay by my side. Listen to what I say!

1811
00:54:09,937 --> 00:54:12,047
Suffering alone...

1812
00:54:12,697 --> 00:54:14,817
Then disappearing by yourself...

1813
00:54:15,617 --> 00:54:16,737
That's...

1814
00:54:17,507 --> 00:54:20,447
That's... That's too selfish!

1815
00:54:24,007 --> 00:54:27,967
There's no need to worry. Daru is my right hand.

1816
00:54:28,797 --> 00:54:30,817
You can rely on him in your hour of need.

1817
00:54:31,657 --> 00:54:35,257
Mayuri might not look it, but she's more mature than you in some respects.

1818
00:54:36,537 --> 00:54:40,677
During the hard times, you can cry on her shoulder. She'll always be there.

1819
00:54:41,417 --> 00:54:44,387
Faris and Rukako will help you too.

1820
00:54:45,307 --> 00:54:46,517
There's nothing to worry about.

1821
00:54:47,517 --> 00:54:51,517
Stupid! That's not what I'm trying to say—

1822
00:55:13,037 --> 00:55:14,387
You're horrible...

1823
00:55:14,887 --> 00:55:18,507
You do something that'll leave a deep imprint in the hippocampus

1824
00:55:18,997 --> 00:55:20,437
and then tell me to forget you?

1825
00:55:20,907 --> 00:55:22,077
You're horrible...

1826
00:55:23,277 --> 00:55:24,247
Sorry...

1827
00:55:24,887 --> 00:55:26,587
You're horrible...

1828
00:55:45,507 --> 00:55:50,287
<i>Okabe didn't contact me at all after that.</i>

1829
00:55:50,967 --> 00:55:53,327
<i>Neither by phone... nor email.</i>

1830
00:55:54,217 --> 00:55:56,307
<i>I didn't contact him, either.</i>

1831
00:55:57,887 --> 00:55:59,967
<i>I didn't know what to say to him.</i>

1832
00:56:01,767 --> 00:56:04,797
<i>I couldn't say anything...</i>

1833
00:56:05,287 --> 00:56:08,177
<i>I'm sure Okabe also felt...</i>

1834
